Package: qsh-client
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends:
 ${misc:Depends},
 ${shlibs:Depends},
Recommends:
 openssh-client,
Suggests:
 qsh-server,
Built-Using: ${cargo:Built-Using}
Static-Built-Using: ${cargo:Static-Built-Using}
X-Cargo-Built-Using: ${cargo:X-Cargo-Built-Using}
Description: remote shell over QUIC whose sessions survive network changes (client)
 qsh is used like ssh: the same destinations, ~/.ssh/config, keys, agent,
 known_hosts and ProxyJump. The first connection runs over ssh to start the
 session; after that the session lives on the server and the client talks to
 it over QUIC, so it survives Wi-Fi/cellular switches, sleep and roaming, and
 replays the output that was missed. When UDP is blocked, qsh falls back to
 TLS over TCP or to an ssh pipe.
 .
 This package contains the client, qsh. The remote host needs qsh-server.

Package: qsh-server
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends:
 ${misc:Depends},
 ${shlibs:Depends},
Recommends:
 openssh-server,
Built-Using: ${cargo:Built-Using}
Static-Built-Using: ${cargo:Static-Built-Using}
X-Cargo-Built-Using: ${cargo:X-Cargo-Built-Using}
Description: remote shell over QUIC whose sessions survive network changes (server)
 qsh is used like ssh: the same destinations, ~/.ssh/config, keys, agent,
 known_hosts and ProxyJump. The first connection runs over ssh to start the
 session; after that the session lives on the server and the client talks to
 it over QUIC, so it survives Wi-Fi/cellular switches, sleep and roaming, and
 replays the output that was missed.
 .
 This package contains qsh-server, which the client starts over ssh as the
 logged-in user. It needs no root, no configuration and no system service;
 a systemd user unit is included for those who want the daemon supervised.
